\par\par \b \uc2 NZ  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Leaves Cash Rate Unchanged At 5.25%  \b0\par\par\uc2 787 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 21:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON (Dow Jones)--The Reserve Bank of New Zealand held the Official Cash Rate steady at 5.25% Thursday, saying it still believes the slowing economy will eventually reduce inflation pressures, but that near-term inflation might prompt a small rate hike later this year.  \par \par \uc2 And in a surprise announcement, the Reserve Bank said it has asked Finance Minster Michael Cullen to be allowed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to influence the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar exchange rate.  \par \par \uc2 Commenting on the reason for leaving the OCR unchanged, Reserve Bank governor Alan Bollard said "with tentative signs becoming more evident in recent weeks, it remains our view that this projected growth slowdown will occur and eventually will reduce the accumulated inflation pressures."  \par \par \uc2 Despite his forecast of a slowdown in the longer-term, Bollard said near-term inflation pressures remain and "the latest activity indicators remain quite robust."  \par \par \uc2 Under an agreement with the government, the Reserve Bank must keep annual inflation inside a 1% to 3% target band.  \par \par \uc2 "There are ongoing risks that the bottlenecks in the economy persist for some time yet," said Bollard in the March Monetary Policy Statement, or MPS.  \par \par \uc2 "Any persistence in the current inflation pressures could see actual inflation nearing the top of the bank's target range."  \par \par \uc2 "With this uncertainty, we judge it as appropriate at this stage to wait and watch the data, to see whether a further small increase in interest rates will be required this year."  \par \par \uc2 Also Thursday, Bollard said he the Reserve Bank has recommended to Finance Minister Michael Cullen that when the New Zealand dollar is "exceptionally and unjustifiably high" that the bank be allowed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.  \par \par \uc2 Using \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars to buy foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  "would put downward pressure on the exchange rate."  \par \par \uc2 When the New Zealand dollar is "exceptionally and unjustifiably low" the Reserve Bank wants to be allowed to buy New Zealand dollars.  \par \par \uc2 Seven of 13 economists in a Dow Jones Newswires poll before the decision expected the Official Cash Rate to be raised to 5.5% and most thought the cash rate wouldn't go higher than 5.5% in 2004.  \par \par \uc2 The Reserve Bank ended a cycle of interest rate cuts with a surprise 25 basis point rate hike in January to 5.25% to keep consumer price inflation in check following stronger-than-expected domestic economic growth.  \par \par \uc2 The bank said at the time that the January hike ensured that any future rate hikes would likely be moderate.  \par \par \uc2 Bollard said Thursday that January's hike was justified because New Zealand's sustained economic growth over recent years has increased inflation pressures in domestic industries, including housing and construction.  \par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, overall inflation has been "so far offset by weak imported inflation due to the rising NZ dollar," he said.  \par \par \uc2 The Consumer Price Index rose only 1.6% from a year earlier in the fourth quarter of 2003, well inside the Reserve Bank's inflation target.  \par \par \uc2 Bank Lowers Long-Term Growth Forecast  \par \par \uc2 In the March MPS, the Reserve Bank upped its near-term growth forecast, but lowered the longer-term forecasts.  \par \par \uc2 The bank is now projecting gross domestic product growth in the 12-months to the end of March at aroudn 3.5%, up from its December MPS estimate of 2.75%.  \par \par \uc2 Growth is then expected to slow to around 3% in 2005 year, unchanged from December's forecast. However in the 2006 year, growth is expected to have slowed considerably to 1.75%, down from its previous projection of 2.5%  \par \par \uc2 The bank also said that "significant inflation pressures have accumulated in parts of the domestic economy" despite signs of a slowdown in growth.  \par \par \uc2 The bank is now projecting annual inflation in the first half of 2004 to average around 2.25%, up from its December expectations of 1.5%. It is expected to remain around 2.5% in the second half of 2004 and the first half of 2005.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Inflation, Monetary Conditions Projections \par  \par                       1H04    2H04    1H05   2H05 \par  \par 90-Day Bill (Avg)     5.50    5.75    5.75   5.75 \par NZD TWI (Avg)        67.75   67.25   64.25  62.25 \par CPI (Annual)          2.25%   2.50%   2.50%  2.75% \par  \par  \par Economic Projections  - March Years \par  \par                       2004    2005    2006 \par  \par GDP                   3.5%    3.00%   1.75% \par Potential Output      3.5%    3.50%   3.50% \par Terms Of Trade        2.0%   -4.75%   0.25% \par Curr Acct % GDP      -5.75%  -6.75%  -6.75% \par World GDP             3.25%   3.75%   3.50% \par World CPI             1.25%   1.50%   1.75% \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Christopher Niesche & Shri Navaratnam, Dow Jones Newswires; 64-4-471-5990; christopher.niesche@dowjones.com [ 10-03-04 2011GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 4333  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040310e03a000zy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
