\par\par \b \uc2 RBNZ non- \highlight22\uc2 intervention currency \highlight\uc2  stance holds-Bollard.   \b0\par\par\uc2 515 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 November 2003\par 01:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON, Nov 27 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is sticking by its policy of non-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets although it is reviewing what tools might be open to it, Governor Alan Bollard said on Thursday.   \par \par \uc2 The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  would only consider \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  under conditions of severe market disruption, where a price was not being given for trades in New Zealand dollars, Bollard told a parliamentary committee.   \par \par \uc2 "That would much more likely be in a case where the New Zealand economy was either under severe stress or there were some big shocks coming through from abroad, and there were some concerns about the New Zealand dollar falling rapidly," Bollard said. The Minister of Finance recently said that he had options to bring down kiwi, which has risen 16 percent on a trade weighted basis and by 30 percent against the U.S. dollar over the past year.   \par \par \uc2 Bollard said the key influence on kiwi at present was financial markets' concern over the size of the United States current account and fiscal deficits, which the RBNZ could do nothing about.   \par \par \uc2 "That's not to say that what the bank does or says can't have an effect, we can under certain circumstances. But we're interested in all of this as the traded sector ought to be in the real exchange rate and the longer term aspects of it because that's what impacts through the New Zealand economy."   \par \par \uc2 However, the level of the exchange rate and the damage it could be causing the tradeable sector was one factor that shaped monetary policy, Bollard said.   \par \par \uc2 The RBNZ has previously cited the high level of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and its impact on exporters as one factor in either cutting or holding its official cash rate.   \par \par \uc2 The bank will issue a monetary policy statement on December 4, with money markets pricing in a 25 basis point rise, but a Reuters poll puts the chances of a hike at 40 percent.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has kept rates steady at 5.0 percent since August, after three rapid fire cuts totalling 75 basis points between April and July.   \par \par \uc2 Most forecasters surveyed expect a hike in the first quarter next year saying that the strength of the New Zealand dollar - estimated at four percent above the RBNZ's forecast for the current period - is offsetting inflationary pressures from the strong domestic economy. Bollard said New Zealand exporters had adjusted much better to kiwi's current high level than in a previous period in the mid-1990s, when it reached $0.72, because they were more aware what tools finance markets were able to offer.   \par \par \uc2 Although he refused to answer questions that might directly relate to next week's monetary statement, Bollard said the strength of the housing sector reflected a "a stronger demographic" and increase in demand because of immigration.   \par \par \uc2 The level of household debt was not a problem for the stability of the financial system, although time would probably show that some of the investment had been misdirected, he said.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031127dzbr0000j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
