\par\par \b \uc2 More upside seen for NZ dollar, despite Cullen.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By James Gray   \par \uc2 645 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 November 2003\par 06:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 AUCKLAND, Nov 7 (Reuters) - The New Zealand dollar is expected to push higher, especially against the backdrop of a weaker U.S. dollar, but will fall short of post-float highs, economists said on Friday.   \par \par \uc2 Kiwi has been one of the stellar performers of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets over the past 18 months as it has benefited from high interest rate differentials, strong economic fundamentals, and U.S. dollar weakness.   \par \par \uc2 Despite concern voiced at the size and speed of the appreciation - 29 percent over the past year against the greenback, and 14 percent on a trade weighted basis - kiwi is not seen as likely to correct any time soon.   \par \par \uc2 For the moment, economists are forecasting the kiwi to rise further, particularly if U.S. dollar weakness persists.   \par \par \uc2 Factors supporting the kiwi include prospects of higher rates after the Reserve Bank of Australia raised rates by 25 basis points to 5.0 percent on Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2 Deutsche Bank NZ expects the kiwi to hit 63 U.S. cents in 12 month's time - a forecast that is likely to be revised upward if U.S. dollar weakness persists.   \par \par \uc2 But the bank sees less upside for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Trade Weighted Index if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  opts to keep rates at 5.0 percent while the RBA continues along a tightening path.   \par \par \uc2 National Bank of NZ (NBNZ) sees the kiwi at $0.6300-$0.6400 in the year ahead, but does not expect it to reach the peak of previous cycles - $0.7275 in June 1988, and $0.7170 in November 1996.   \par \par \uc2 Kiwi is currently sitting near year highs at $0.6138/48.   \par \par \uc2 "Our 12 month view of 63 U.S. cents is likely to be revised upward, but less upside is seen for the Trade-Weighted Index, particularly if the Reserve Bank of NZ remains on hold while the RBA continues to tighten," Deutsche Bank NZ senior economist Darren Gibbs said.   \par \par \uc2 JAWBONING   \par \par \uc2 Recent comments talk by the Finance Minister, Michael Cullen, about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and possible government options to curb its rise have been largely dismissed as just talk.   \par \par \uc2 Cullen, who has in the past tried to talk down the value of the kiwi to soften the blow to \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -prone export sector, raised the spectre of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  before parliament's finance and expenditure committee meeting this week.   \par \par \uc2 Questioned further in parliament, Cullen said: "My intention was to signal that the government is not without options. No specific conclusions can be drawn from that statement, and it would be most unwise to do so".   \par \par \uc2 Since floating in 1985 successive governments and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  have taken a hands-off approach to the New Zealand dollar, leaving the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to find its own level.   \par \par \uc2 Over that period kiwi has traded a 38.95 to 72.75 U.S. cent range.   \par \par \uc2 Cullen has so far declined to elaborate on what the "options" might be, but his comment was nevertheless seen as a departure from the status quo.   \par \par \uc2 "Cullen is clearly trying to jawbone. He's been trying it now for months, using different tacks, now he's floating the idea of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Darren Gibbs, senior economist at Deutsche Bank NZ.   \par \par \uc2 Basil Payn, head of spot \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Westpac Institutional Bank, said the market saw Cullen's comments as representing a slight change in tack.   \par \par \uc2 "It's a change of 'speak' but I don't think the market will see it as any change in policy," Payn said.   \par \par \uc2 There were other forms of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , other than selling kiwi, which could dampen the economy and the exchange rate, a such as a capital gains tax on property, Gibbs said.   \par \par \uc2 Such a tax, while difficult to enforce, could help slow the housing market, reducing the need for the high interest rates that have helped underpin the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031107dzb700109\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
