\par\par \b \uc2 NZ FinMin avoids  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  "options" questions.  \b0\par\par\uc2 385 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 November 2003\par 04:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON, Nov 6 (Reuters) - New Zealand Finance Minister Michael Cullen on Thursday refused to amplify on comments about government options to curb the rise in the kiwi dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Cullen was asked in Parliament by opposition National Party leader Don Brash, a former \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor, to expand on comments made the previous day that the government was not without options to combat the rise in the New Zealand dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "My intention was to signal that the government is not without options, but no specific conclusions can be drawn from that statement and it would be most unwise to do so," Cullen said.  \par \par \uc2 Pressed by Brash and other lawmakers to give more detail Cullen would only repeat the answer.  \par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar was trading a narrow range around $0.6160/65, near six year highs, as it kept track with the closely correlated Australian dollar .  \par \par \uc2 The Aussie was lifted on Wednesday by a surprise rate increase by the Reserve Bank of Australia, which brought its cash rate to 5.0 percent, the same as New Zealand.  \par \par \uc2 So far this year kiwi has risen around 18 percent against the greenback and 10 percent on a trade weighted basis .  \par \par \uc2 Howver, on the cross rate against the Aussie kiwi is down around seven percent this year, and it has also underperformed the Aussie's gain of around 27 percent against the greenback this year.  \par \par \uc2 Cullen remarked that a rise in the kiwi on Wednesday as a result of the RBA's rate rise was an example of the "irrational exuberance" of financial markets.  \par \par \uc2 He has periodically commented on the high level of kiwi and the damage it has been inflicting on the external sector, and strategists have characterised his most recent comments as further "jawboning".  \par \par \uc2 The government has the ability to direct the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBNZ) to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, but both have steered clear of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since kiwi was floated in 1985.  \par \par \uc2 The bank's monetary policy instructions from the government, renewed last year when governor Alan Bollard took office, say it should avoid "unnecessary instability" in exchange rates.  \par \par \uc2 New Zealand did not have the level of resources used by Japanese authorities in trying to affect the value of the yen, Cullen said.  \par \par \uc2 ($1=NZ$1.64).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031106dzb6000hn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
