\par\par \b \uc2 NZ FinMin weighs options on resurgent kiwi dollar.  \b0\par\par\uc2 463 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 November 2003\par 03:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  WELLINGTON, Nov 5 (Reuters) - New Zealand's Finance Minister said on Wednesday the government had options to influence the value of the kiwi dollar, which has soared to six year highs against the greenback, although he declined to give details.   \par \par \uc2  Michael Cullen, who has repeatedly said the high level of the kiwi dollar is hurting exporters, was asked at a parliamentary committee if he intended to do anything about its value, New Zealand Press Association (NZPA) reported.   \par \par \uc2  "The government doesn't regard itself as entirely without options in this respect, but those options have to be dealt with very carefully indeed," Cullen was quoted as saying.   \par \par \uc2  He did not outline the options and declined to comment on any timeframe.   \par \par \uc2  "I'm always considering all sorts of things. When I'm doing something in that area is probably only for me to know at the moment," Cullen added.   \par \par \uc2  Neither the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  government nor the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to affect the value of the New Zealand dollar since the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was floated in 1985.   \par \par \uc2  However, Cullen has periodically commented on the high level of kiwi and the damage it has been inflicting on the external sector.   \par \par \uc2  SELF CORRECT   \par \par \uc2  Cullen predicted the New Zealand dollar, which has gained around 25 percent against the U.S. dollar over the past 12 months on the back of relatively high interest rates and a robust domestic economy, could correct of its own accord.   \par \par \uc2  "Who knows, there might be a correction in the U.S. dollar value, though I think it is more likely looking out, that the New Zealand dollar will correct on its own, given the widening current account deficit," Cullen said.   \par \par \uc2  Weaker exports and the SARS virus combined with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's climb pushed New Zealand's current account deficit to its highest annual level in three years at the end of the June quarter to NZ$5.89 billion ($3.59 billion) or 4.6 percent of gross domestic product.   \par \par \uc2  It was not clear whether lowering New Zealand's official cash rate would automatically subdue the kiwi dollar as it sometimes encouraged people to put money into the country in the assumption that a cut would lead to higher economic growth, NZPA quoted Cullen as saying.   \par \par \uc2  Cullen's comments came shortly before Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised interest rates by a quarter percentage point to 5.0 percent - the same level as New Zealand's benchmark lending rate.   \par \par \uc2  In response, the Australian dollar  gained nearly one percent against the greenback before profit takers pegged it back to around $0.7090. Kiwi last traded around $0.6130, up from $0.6120 at the start of Wednesday's local session.   \par \par \uc2  ($1=NZ$1.64).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031105dzb5000bc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
