\par\par \b \uc2  New Zealand's  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  joins Swiss-based BIS. \b0\par\par\uc2 191 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 August 2003\par 01:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WELLINGTON, Aug 19 (Reuters) - The Reserve Bank of New Zealand said Friday it had joined the Bank for International Settlements (BIS), the Switzerland-based bank and forum for central banks which provides emergency cash. \par \par \uc2  BIS currently has 49 member central banks, including those of Australia, China, Germany, France, Britain and the United States, plus the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The RBNZ said it had paid NZ$100 million ($59 million) for a small shareholding in the Basel-based institution, which provides emergency funds to support the global monetary system when needed. \par \par \uc2  "The benefits to both the Reserve Bank and New Zealand include increased access to foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  funding, which in a crisis could enhance the bank's capabilities to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets to maintain stability," the bank said in a statement. \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar was floated nearly 20 years ago. \par \par \uc2  In the 1960s BIS helped support the Italian lira, French franc, and British pound, and more recently provided cash to help stabilise the economies of Mexico and Brazil. \par \par \uc2  ($1=NZ$1.71). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030818dz8i0192u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
