\par\par \b \uc2  NZ Dlr Lower Late; Profit-Taking After Cullen Comments \b0\par\par\uc2 506 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Mai 2002\par 08:36\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  WELLINGTON -(Dow Jones)- The New Zealand dollar was lower against the U.S. dollar late Friday in Wellington after a volatile session marked by profit-taking in the local unit after its recent strong gains. \par \par \uc2  At 0625 GMT, the New Zealand dollar was quoted at US$0.4680, down from US$0.4730 late Thursday in Wellington and down also from a 23-month high at US$0.4765 traded in New York prior to the open. \par \par \uc2  A Wellington bank dealer said the pair traded a very wide range of about US$0.4660 and US$0.4735 Friday. \par \par \uc2  Comments by Finance Minister Michael Cullen early in the session - in which he expressed concern about the potential economic implications should the recent strong run in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continue indefinitely - sparked an initial selloff in the New Zealand dollar down to about US$0.4680 compared with US$0.4725 prior to his comments, the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The dealer said it was speculative accounts which had recently become long in the New Zealand dollar who availed the opportunity of Cullen's comments to sell-down the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a profit. \par \par \uc2  Cullen said in interviews with newswire services that the recent strength in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be of concern to him if the trend were to continue, although economists and market-watchers were quick to note that in New Zealand the government doesn't have anything to do with setting the level of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , moreover, hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar became a free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the mid 1980s. \par \par \uc2  Almost everyone expects the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to resume its appreciating trend as the year progresses, if for no other reason than interest rate differentials, which strongly favor the New Zealand dollar over other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers said overnight they expect buying interest will lend a base of support to the New Zealand dollar on any dip toward US$0.4650. Selling interest will cap any gains toward US$0.4720. \par \par \uc2  One dealer said any breach of US$0.4650, however, could see a quick march down to US$0.4600. \par \par \uc2  Statistics New Zealand will Monday publish its provisional April merchandise trade report. \par \par \uc2  According to a Dow Jones Newswires poll of economists, the economy recorded a merchandise trade surplus of around NZ$275.5 million in April compared with a surplus of NZ$456 million in March. \par \par \uc2  Against the Australian dollar, the New Zealand dollar was quoted at A$0.8444, down from A$0.8492 late Thursday in Wellington. \par \par \uc2  In his comments Friday, Cullen noted the cross rate. Any sustained rise in the cross toward A$0.8500 would likely cause concern to local exporters, the minister told Dow Jones Newswires. \par \par \uc2  Despite the easing in the cross rate Friday, it remains up about 1.6% from A$0.8310 at the start of May. \par \par \uc2  -By Chris Ritchie, Dow Jones Newswires; 644 4715990; chris.ritchie@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1062 20711 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020524dy5o000co\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
