\par\par \b \uc2  Can$ snaps winning streak as tech worries weigh. \b0\par\par\uc2 293 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juni 2001\par 13:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 12 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar broke a week-long winning streak against the greenback on Tuesday, as a raft of earnings warnings in the semiconductor sector stoked concern over Canada's economy. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar retreated almost a cent from the previous day's four-month highs around C$1.5150 , underperforming its commodity-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peers. \par \par \uc2  Recent optimism over Canada's growth outlook waned late on Monday after an analyst cut estimates for technology bellwether Nortel Networks Corp and fresh earnings warnings from two U.S. semiconductor firms. Toronto stocks closed down 1.45 percent. \par \par \uc2  "The Canadian dollar is being hurt by its vulnerability to the U.S. economy and losses in tech stocks," said Jim Webber, chief economist at Toronto Dominion Securities in London. \par \par \uc2  "This is coming at a time when there is growing confidence that Australian and New Zealand economies are on a recovery track." \par \par \uc2  Around 80 percent of Canada's exports go to the United States. Canada's economy and its stock market are heavily exposed to the high-tech sector. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the Australian dollar - which had survived a test of US$0.52 in the Asian session - gained over half a percent against the U.S. dollar, supported by the euro's rebound amid persistent wariness of euro-supportive \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar also tracked the Aussie higher, coming within firing range of US$0.42 , a key near-term chart barrier. \par \par \uc2  "Recent Australian and New Zealand economic data have surprised on the upside," said Jane Foley, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Barclays Capital. "Both the Aussie and the Kiwi dollars have also gained from the bounce in euro/dollar, while the Canadian dollar has been left struggling." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010712dx6c01ws6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
