\par\par \b \uc2  A$ rebounds from record low vs US dollar in Europe. \b0\par\par\uc2 528 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Oktober 2000\par 12:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Oct 6 (Reuters) - The Australian dollar rebounded in Europe on Friday from record lows it set against the U.S. dollar earlier in Sydney, helped by the euro's recovery against the U.S. unit amid concerns of further Group of Seven \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar outperformed the Australian dollar and held above record lows around US$0.3990 it had hit against the U.S. dollar on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The latest push down in the Aussie to record lows around US$0.5280 in Friday's Sydney comes after the Reserve Bank of Australia's decision earlier in the week to leave its interests on hold despite market expectations of a quarter point tightening. \par \par \uc2  The renewed selling in the Aussie dollar coincided with a downturn in the euro after the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  surprised markets by raising interest rates on Thursday, raising concerns over euro zone growth prospects. \par \par \uc2  However, the euro's rebound across the board in Europe on Friday helped lift the Aussie dollar, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "There has been a link between the euro and the Australian dollar," said Andrew Baxter, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the Commonwealth Bank of Australia in London. \par \par \uc2  "The RBA passed a rate hike this week. Also important to look at is the reserves positions - while the ECB has a large warchest, the RBA does not and that is why the Aussie dollar is open to a speculative attack. So it has been a proxy hedge for the euro because people think that the Aussie won't get that much help from the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the euro." \par \par \uc2  The Aussie rose as far as US$0.5338 by 1012 GMT according to Reuters data, up about 1/2 a cent from earlier record lows around US$0.5280, as euro/dollar firmed . \par \par \uc2  KIWI DOLLAR OUTPERFORMS AUSSIE DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  Against the kiwi dollar, the Aussie fell to new session lows around NZ$1.3230 . \par \par \uc2  The New Zealand dollar also recovered to day's highs around US$0.4030, up more than a quarter cent from the day's lows, which were just above Wednesday's record lows around US$0.3990. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the kiwi dollar outperformed the Australian dollar mainly because the US$0.40 level was seen as a psychologically important level which the market was reluctant to break below. \par \par \uc2  The market's focus was now shifting to the U.S. payrolls report due out at 1230 GMT, particularly after the U.S. Federal Reserve warned this week that a tight labour market was an inflationary threat. \par \par \uc2  Chartists said if the Australian dollar could fall to US$0.50 if it broke below the US$0.5280 record lows. \par \par \uc2  "The unit has lower to go and ideally for now US$0.5340 will cap the upside for a test of US$0.5250," said Rahul Badhwar, senior marketing manager at HSBC, Bombay. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar was mired in narrow ranges near recent highs around 1.4930/40 per U.S. dollar, awaiting the release of the Canadian September jobs data due out at 1100 GMT and the U.S. payrolls figures. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dwa6057sn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
