\par\par \b \uc2  NZ Dlr Slightly Up Late; Market Awaits Key Overnight Events \b0\par\par\uc2 682 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Oktober 2000\par 07:03\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  WELLINGTON -(Dow Jones)- The New Zealand dollar was knocked back by a late sell order Thursday, but still managed to close slightly higher against the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Around 0440 GMT (12:40 a.m. EDT) the New Zealand dollar was quoted at US$0.4020, up from US$0.4013 late Wednesday in Wellington, but down from its local open at US$0.4049. \par \par \uc2  During Wednesday's session it traded at a record low of US$0.3992, taking it under its previous low of US$0.4015 traded on Sept. 21, 2000. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  came under pressure late in the day from a sell order versus the U.S. dollar and some New Zealand/Australian selling. \par \par \uc2  They said the market showed little reaction to comments by New Zealand \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Don Brash in a speech titled: "The fall of the New Zealand dollar: Why has it happened, and what does it mean?". \par \par \uc2  The speech gave no basis for a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they said. "He's not saying we're going to do this or do that to support the economy, and keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher," one dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The New Zealand \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded in a range of US$0.4020 to US$0.4058 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  One dealer said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had dropped to the closing level from around US$0.4040 in a short period of around 15 minutes. \par \par \uc2  "There's been a bit of an offshore flow of New Zealand dollar selling. It's been very quiet the rest of the day," he said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market will watch both European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Bank of England meetings overnight. \par \par \uc2  "(They) have their council meetings where no rate change is expected from either. The risks for a rate hike are higher for the ECB, however we expect them to hold off until October 19," one market watcher said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Another dealer said he wouldn't be surprised if the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central\par Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  does hike rates, to support recent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in favor of the\par euro. In recent months the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 New Zealand\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollar has been influenced by the\par direction of both Australian dollar and euro.\par - - 05/10/00 05-03G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Another dealer said the New Zealand dollar could reach a historic low\par overnight, but added chances are about even it will stay above\par Wednesday's low.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par He said there is initial support at US$0.4010/20, with further support\par at US$0.3980.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dealer said he was interested in comments Brash made in is speech\par regarding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 New Zealand\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollar. Brash\par said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would only \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par market if trading in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 New Zealand\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollar was disorderly, but that\par wasn't the case at present.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dealer said for most of the session the New Zealand dollar was\par bought on the cross rate with the neighboring Australian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ,\par however late in the session there was selling on the cross rate. At 0440\par GMT the local dollar was quoted at A$0.7506, up from A$0.7483 late\par Wednesday and higher than A$0.7534 early Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On a cross rate with the Japanese yen, the local dollar was quoted at\par Y43.91, up from Y43.64 late Wednesday and higher than Y44.27 early\par Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Against its trade weighted index, the New Zealand dollar is 46.72, up\par from 46.57 late Wednesday and lower than 47.04 early Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Around 0330 GMT the Reserve Bank's Monetary Conditions Index, which is\par a composite measure of the trade-weighted index measure of the New\par Zealand dollar and yields on 90-day bank bills, was at minus-1000.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  assumes the MCI, in nominal terms, will be about an\par average of minus-300 in the first half of 2000, then about an average of\par minus-75 for the second half of 2000.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Alan Wood; Dow Jones Newswires; 64 4 801 8960;\par alan.wood@dowjones.com\par -0- 05/10/00 05-12G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1062 20711 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dwa504ur0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
