\par\par \b \uc2  Aussie dollar pulls back in Europe, eyes euro. \b0\par\par\uc2 459 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 September 2000\par 13:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 26 (Reuters) - The Australian dollar backed off early highs against the U.S. dollar in Europe on Tuesday, disappointed by the euro's inability to extend gains after last week's bounce on concerted Group of Seven \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  A dearth of domestic economic indicators in Australia this week meant the market continued to focus on the euro, which the Aussie dollar has been tracking for some time. And so far the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was struggling to make headway after Friday's spike up in reaction to G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said. \par \par \uc2  Until U.S. businesses and securities loose lustre in the eyes of global investors, gains in the euro and dollar bloc \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were likely to be tentative despite the risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "I think both the Australian and \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars will just continue to be disadvantaged versus the U.S. dollar unless there's a key change in U.S. fundamentals," said Jane Foley, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Barclays Capital in London. \par \par \uc2  "Trends are a function of capital flows and capital flows are a function of where investors want to invest their money, and that in turn is a function of fundamentals. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by itself cannot change trends." \par \par \uc2  Foley expected the Australian dollar to continue gyrating in line with euro/dollar's swings in the near term, given the nervousness about euro \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar pulled back below US$0.5470 by 1005 GMT after briefly rising above US$0.55 in Sydney trading. It was now about a cent off peaks it hit immediately after the euro \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday and back within 1-1/2 cents of record lows around US$0.5360 set last week. \par \par \uc2  "There are a lot of people who went long the Aussie dollar after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (in the euro), and they may have to be shaken out before we can get through these levels above US$0.55," said a Japanese bank dealer in London. \par \par \uc2  The New Zealand dollar was also on the retreat, fading toward US$0.4120 from early highs above US$0.4155 to mirror its Australian counterpart. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the day, August trade data released by Statistics New Zealand showed a monthly trade deficit of NZ$345 million, in line with market expectations. \par \par \uc2  While crude oil imports were a major reason for the deficit and the figures were in line with economists' forecasts, traders said the data were a reminder of New Zealand's bulging current account imbalance and certainly not helping mend sentiment on the kiwi dollar. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar was trapped in a tight C$1.4863-1.4880 range, near the middle of its two-week trading band. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9q06ddj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
