\par\par \b \uc2  NZ's PM Clark Says NZ Dollar Undervalued \b0\par\par\uc2 536 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 September 2000\par 08:31\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  CANBERRA -(Dow Jones)- New Zealand Prime Minister Helen Clark said Tuesday that the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is undervalued but coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by central banks last week to support the euro has helped the New Zealand dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The money has flooded into the greenback and the yen," she said during an interview with Australian Broadcasting Corp. radio. "The Aussie, the Kiwi, the euro, we are all struggling in its wake." \par \par \uc2  "The best we can do is say yes, our \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are undervalued but we are going to get out there and boost our exporting and our tourism like crazy to take advantage of that in the mean time," she said. \par \par \uc2  At 0642 GMT, the New Zealand dollar was quoted at US$0.4147, up from US$0.4111 late Monday in Wellington and up also from its local open at around US$0.4130. \par \par \uc2  Clark said the coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the world's major central banks last Friday to support the euro has helped the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar but should be followed up with further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Asked whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be effective, she said "it will be if they do it a number more times but to date it has helped our dollar a bit." \par \par \uc2  Clark, who was speaking from Auckland, is heading to Australia for talks with Australian Prime Minister John Howard. The talks are scheduled for Thursday. \par \par \uc2  She also said that a common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for Australia and New Zealand is inevitable because of a global trend toward larger and larger population groupings. \par \par \uc2  Asked whether a common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is part of the formal agenda for her talks with Howard, she said there is "nothing formal but it is obviously something that has been batted about." \par \par \uc2  Clark said New Zealand's government has "got to look at (a common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) to see whether there are advantages for New Zealanders. If it helps us maintain prosperity we would be crazy to say no." \par \par \uc2  "I am of the view myself that the way the world is going, we are looking for larger and larger critical mass," she said. New Zealand is a small country with a population of 3.8 million and Australia, with a population of nearly 19 million "isn't a very big country either," Clark said. \par \par \uc2  "So we are all looking at how we can increase the mass in which we work," she added. \par \par \uc2  Australian Treasurer Peter Costello has said Australia would only consider a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  union where New Zealand takes on the Australian dollar and wouldn't consider a common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "We take a rather different view," Clark said. "The proposals that have been batted around by the economists in New Zealand have talked about a merger and a common \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  "I think the Australian government, like our own government, really hasn't given it serious consideration," she said. \par \par \uc2  "Personally, I think becoming part of larger critical masses is inevitable," Clark said. "Our stock markets are looking at merging, there is this issue over the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and of course the New Zealand and Australian economies are very, very closely integrated." \par \par \uc2  -By Iain McDonald, Dow Jones Newswires; 61-2-6208-0901; iain.mcdonald@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw9q04pwl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
