\par\par \b \uc2  NZ dlr rebounds after sinking to fresh record low. \b0\par\par\uc2 636 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 September 2000\par 23:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2   --------------------------------------------------------------- \par \par \uc2   * DAY'S RANGE * SUPPORT * RESTNCE * RSI-27 * MA-10  * MA-20 \par \par \uc2   * 0.4045-4100 * 0.4020  * 0.4100  * 42 PCT * 0.4056 * 0.4073 \par \par \uc2   ----------------VIEWS FROM THE MARKET (Sept 21)--------------- \par \par \uc2      * NZD rebounds after sinking to fresh record low of $0.4045 \par \par \uc2      * NZ dlr flow driven in thin trade, coattails weaker AUD \par \par \uc2   * Possible retracement in Aussie, euro may support kiwi. \par \par \uc2  WELLINGTON, Sept 21 (Reuters) - The New Zealand dollar bounced back from its new record low set overnight following the plunge in the euro and the Australian dollar . \par \par \uc2  While momentarily flirting with its record low level in early Thursday morning trade, the kiwi was hanging around $0.4070 level by 9 a.m. (2100 GMT), after its record low of $0.4050. \par \par \uc2  "It's totally flow driven if someone comes in and sells it, there's no reason we can't go lower and if someone comes in and buys it, there's no reason we won't go higher," one \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader said. \par \par \uc2  "The market is purely at the hands of transactional flows." \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the kiwi dollar was basically coattailing Australian dollar and the euro. \par \par \uc2  The euro/dollar emerged from its record low below 84.40 cents by late New York trading after U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers said the U.S. dollar policy was unchanged and the U.S. was watching \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  developments closely. \par \par \uc2  "It's really out of our hands whether we go up or down. The market is quite thin and volatile," the trader said. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar's move was seen dictating the kiwi's direction in local trade, and some picked retracement from losses in the Australasian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Reserve Bank of Australia. \par \par \uc2  "There's still that perception that that's the risk, so therefore it gives it a slightly bit of bid tone, unless we see fresh selling. Without fresh selling, Aussie will be bid," the trader said. \par \par \uc2  "At this stage, I would say there is some risk to both kiwi and Aussie just spiking up quickly. But having said that, if it gets up to the 41 figure level, at this stage, you'll have to say it's probably sell." \par \par \uc2  On the cross, the kiwi was quoted at $0.7543/63 at 9 a.m. (2100 GMT) against $0.7580/00 on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Another \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader said the Group of Seven finance ministers' meeting scheduled for the coming weekend was keeping the market on its toes. \par \par \uc2  "They will be talking about the euro, what has to be done, strong dollar policy, whether or not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be recommended. International Monetary Fund has recommended \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  into the euro market and whether or not the G7 will go along with it, we'll wait and see," he said. \par \par \uc2  "There has to be a reasonable chance that we'll see that come, come Monday morning." \par \par \uc2  IMF Managing Director Horst Koehler said on Wednesday the euro was "heavily undervalued" and that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  should not be seen as taboo. \par \par \uc2  Domestically, Statistics \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  will announce its June quarter balance of payments data on Friday at 10.45 a.m. (2245 GMT). New Zealand's large current account deficit is seen as another factor capping any kiwi rally. \par \par \uc2  The current account deficit for the March year had previously been at 8.2 percent of the GDP, but has recently been revised down to 7.1 percent. \par \par \uc2  "If the current dismal state of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is in part attributable to external account comparisons, then there should be reason for change," ANZ said in its commentary. \par \par \uc2  Economists polled by Reuters on average see June quarter balance of payments at NZ$1.353 billion deficit and June year deficit at NZ$7.485 billion, or 7.1 percent of GDP. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9k078fg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
