\par\par \b \uc2  RBA wins battered Australian dollar no respite. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sabrina Ghani \par \uc2 637 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 September 2000\par 15:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 7 (Reuters) - The Australian dollar fell to within sight of its record lows against the U.S. dollar on Thursday, crashing through the levels at which London traders had spotted the Reserve Bank of Australia buying the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  London traders said on Thursday they saw the RBA buying Australian dollars for U.S. dollars below US$0.5600. An RBA official in London declined to comment. \par \par \uc2  However, the size of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's purchases were said to be small and the Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was back on the slide as soon as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  disappeared from view. \par \par \uc2  "The market is not particularly rational now but it could remain that way for quite some time, so to call a turning point for these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is extremely dangerous," said Jim Webber, chief economist at Toronto Dominion Securities in London. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar fell as far as US$0.5535, its lowest level since August 1998 and within sights of record lows around US$0.5530. \par \par \uc2  "It's interesting that they (the RBA) were asking prices yesterday and today they have been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , buying small amounts," said at a trader at a Australian bank in London. "But people are getting used to them being around and it was only small amounts and I still expect it to go to US$0.5330 in the next 24 hours or so." \par \par \uc2  Traders said in an environment where the U.S. dollar remained the market's favourite \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Australian dollar would struggle to find few friends even though economic data remains relatively robust. \par \par \uc2  "It's a function of very, very bad sentiment because the economic data has not been very bad. It's been the same story that international investors would rather hold U.S. dollars than the euro, the Aussie dollar and just about everything else," said Jane Foley, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Barclays Capital. \par \par \uc2  "Our forecast is that the Aussie dollar will go down to US$0.50 in three-months." \par \par \uc2  There was little respite for the Australian dollar from any front and it fell to its lowest in more than five years against the yen as the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  soared across the board, hitting record highs against the euro. \par \par \uc2  The Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's credibility was not being called into question despite its failure to support its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday, with traders saying it had little chance of turning sentiment on its own given current market conditions. \par \par \uc2  "It's disastrous because the interbank market went long when the RBA came in because they expected it to hold the Aussie up and soon as RBA disappeared as they went home, all the bids disappeared. When you have have got markets of this nature you can't stop it," said a trader at a German bank in London. \par \par \uc2  "The only way the Aussie dollar and the Kiwi dollar could get back is if we get the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in euro/dollar. And if ECB come in they will not come alone and they will come with the Bank of Japan \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  the dollar/yen or euro/yen and the RBA would sort out its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  Traders said they did not expect the U.S. Federal Reserve or the Bank of England to join in in any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar dropped to fresh record lows around US$0.4165 against the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the Kiwi dollar would also continue to fall, not only because of the weakness in Aussie dollar but also because its own poor domestic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  "The Kiwi dollar has got even more problems and it is going to continue to underperform and we expect it to fall to US$0.37 in three months," said Foley from Barclays. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9708tvw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
