\par\par \b \uc2  NZ dlr teeters at 14-year low, bond spreads widen. \b0\par\par\uc2 751 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Mai 2000\par 08:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2   ---------------May 11 snapshot at 5 p.m. (0500 GMT)---------- \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab   figures in brackets are from May 10\par \f28 \par \par \uc2   90-DAY BILLS(JUN)  93.17 (PREV 93.16)  90-DAYS  6.70 (PREV \par \par \uc2  6.69) \par \par \uc2   90-DAY BILLS(SEP)  92.89 (PREV 92.88)  04/03    7.02 (PREV \par \par \uc2  7.05) \par \par \uc2   90-DAY BILLS(DEC)  92.69 (PREV 92.65)  11/06    7.03 (PREV \par \par \uc2  7.08) \par \par \uc2   NZD TRADE WEIGHTED 54.06 (PREV 54.17)  07/09    7.03 (PREV \par \par \uc2  7.09) \par \par \uc2   NZD/USD   0.4759/66  (PREV 0.4782/89)  11/11    6.97 (PREV \par \par \uc2  7.03) \par \par \uc2   NZD/AUD   0.8192/12  (PREV 0.8215/34)  US 5-YR  6.63 (PREV \par \par \uc2  6.75) \par \par \uc2   NZD/JPY   52.12/22    (PREV 52.22/32)  US 10-YR 6.40 (PREV \par \par \uc2  6.53) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  RBNZ MCI\tab \tab  -302 (PREV -293)  AU 10-YR 6.45 (PREV\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 6.54)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  ***SPREADS: NZ/US 10-YR +62 (+56)\tab NZ/AUS 10-YR +58\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (+55)***.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par WELLINGTON, May 11 (Reuters) - The New Zealand dollar hit a fresh a 14-year low of US$0.4745 on Thursday after being taken down by the ailing Aussie, which weakened short-end debt.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Bond spreads widened as yields adopted their familiar pattern of underperforming U.S. rallies.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Flows took the kiwi to the new low of early on Thursday, following the previous 14-year low at US$0.4765 24 hours earlier.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par It had since traded up to just over 48 U.S. cents until the further dip on Thursday morning at the local open, followed by a move up to US$0.4780 on Australian jobs data and a shift back to US$0.4760/67 by 4.55 p.m (0455 GMT).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par However, comments from Finance Minister Michael Cullen's office that the kiwi drop was function of U.S. dollar strength saw the kiwi breach support to trade at US$0.4745 at 5.30 p.m. (0530 GMT).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The minister has noted that the Trade-Weighted Index is around 54 and has said what we are talking about here is less to do with New Zealand than with the U.S. dollar going up," a spokeswoman for Finance Minister Michael Cullen told Reuters.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 New Zealand\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  does not \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market but reserves the right to do so.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Cullen's clear on not \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," said one dealer. "But it added to the noise and helped take it lower," he said, adding the key driver was the Aussie dollar's slump.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Cullen is on record as preferring any tightening in monetary conditions to be delivered via interest rates rather than the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Traders expected an overnight range around US$0.4720-90, depending on the levels achieved by the Australian dollar . Bond spreads widened as local maturities retained their pattern of outperforming U.S. weakness and underperforming U.S. rallies, dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The benchmark July 2009s rallied six basis points to close at 7.03 percent and pressure on short-end debt from the sagging kiwi took its toll on the flattening curve.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Physical 90-day bank bills closed one bp weaker at 6.70 percent as the market continues to price in the expected 50 bp hike from the Reserve Bank of New Zealand (RBNZ) at its next rate review on Wednesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Some market economists said the weak kiwi was unlikely to alter the RBNZ's rate views and would simply add another layer of argument for an aggressive tightening next week.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Others are picking a quarter-point hike on the basis of a flow of soft economic data in recent weeks.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par National Bank of NZ economist Cameron Bagrie played down the impact of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on the rate decision.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Although the kiwi has lost against the U.S. dollar, the Trade Weighted Index was broadly in line with RBNZ projections due to weaker euro, Australian dollar and the yen, he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Deutsche Bank senior economist Darren Gibbs said the bank expected the RBNZ to move by 50 basis points and assigned only a 15 percent probability of the RBNZ opting for a 25 bp hike.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "We think that the RBNZ will regard an OCR of 6.5 percent as broadly 'neutral', though the weakness of the NZD means that overall monetary conditions will remain accommodative," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ((Marion Rae, Wellington newsroom, +64 4 471 4277, fax +64 4 473 6212; wellington.newsroom@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 2000.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw5b00ny3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
