\par\par \b \uc2  NZ's Brash Says Monetary Inquiry Is Useful, Appropriate \b0\par\par\uc2 718 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Mai 2000\par 01:06\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  WELLINGTON -(Dow Jones)- Reserve Bank of New Zealand Governor Don Brash said Tuesday a government-sponsored inquiry into monetary policy is an appropriate way of assessing if there are ways to improve either his \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's governance structure, or its \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedures. \par \par \uc2  "I am keen to cooperate fully with the review because I think it is a useful thing to do a decade down the track and if we can find ways of improving the performance of the bank I would certainly welcome that," Brash told reporters. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  independence and legal requirement to aim monetary policy at the maintenance of price stability has been a feature of New Zealand's monetary policy framework for over ten years. \par \par \uc2  "In an important sense the review is a review of how we have performed in the last decade and to the extent that I have carried responsibility for the Bank's decisions in that period then clearly the review is partly of my performance," Brash said. \par \par \uc2  Brash said the official cash rate, which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  uses to implement its monetary policy decisions, is working well so far. \par \par \uc2  "I think we are using the best instrument currently, namely the official cash rate, but of course that has been in place for only about a little over a year. We were using other forms of instruments prior to that and I think it is worthwhile looking at whether that was a sensible evolution," he said. \par \par \uc2  "There are those who feel that we should be using other forms of instruments, such as for example reserve asset ratios of direct kinds of quantitative restrictions on lending. I personally don't think those make sense, but lets have an expert internationally make a judgment on that," he said. \par \par \uc2  Similarly, Brash said the international experience suggests that it isn't very useful for central banks to directly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, but there can't be any harm in the monetary inquiry offering its views on the issue. \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't used its reserves to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since 1985, and the country's political parties also oppose the idea of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Michael Cullen, speaking to reporters with Brash, reiterated that viewpoint Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "I would be very surprised if the reviewer would have in mind to recommend that we went into direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and it would take an awful lot of convincing of me to suggest that we ought to be doing that," Cullen said. \par \par \uc2  Cullen said he expects to shortly announce who will chair or conduct the monetary inquiry. \par \par \uc2  He had decided to publish the inquiry's term of reference before the announcement of a chairman because he wanted financial markets to know what the inquiry is all about prior to Brash's departure on an overseas trip where he will meet investors and other players with an interest in New Zealand markets. \par \par \uc2  Cullen said Brash is traveling overseas shortly. \par \par \uc2  Cullen said one of the issues likely to be canvassed by the monetary inquiry is the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's governance structures. \par \par \uc2  Currently, Governor Brash is personally accountable for maintaining price stability under the terms of the Policy Targets Agreement he signed with Cullen last December. \par \par \uc2  The board of the Reserve Bank, which is chaired by Brash, monitors Brash's performance, but doesn't have input into monetary policy decision-making. \par \par \uc2  Brash told reporters there is a contradiction in the law, which has been recognized for long time, in having a board that he chairs acting as a monitor of his performance. \par \par \uc2  "It may well be that the reviewer (of the monetary inquiry) says that is a contradiction which should be changed," Brash said. \par \par \uc2  Cullen told reporters the role of the Reserve Bank's board is "quite unusual" by international standards. \par \par \uc2  He said the U.S. and U.K. central banks have more "openly collegial" decision-making structures than New Zealand's, and it is appropriate for the inquiry to examine if this should remain so. \par \par \uc2  Cullen earlier Tuesday listed six issues to be reviewed by the upcoming inquiry into monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  -By Chris Ritchie; 64 4 4719058; critchie@ap.org \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw58029qd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
