\par\par \b \uc2  NZ Brash: Most People Now Endorse Jan Cash Rate Rise \b0\par\par\uc2 475 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Februar 2000\par 22:29\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  WELLINGTON -(Dow Jones)- Reserve Bank of New Zealand Governor Don Brash said Wednesday that he believes most people now endorse the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's surprise move in mid-January to raise official cash rates. \par \par \uc2  Brash told a parliamentary select committee that the outlook for world growth and inflation pressures was much different in January, when the decision was made, than in November when the bank completed its economic forecasts. \par \par \uc2  Brash was appearing before the committee to discuss the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's briefing paper to the incoming government, but questions were asked about the increase in the official cash rate to 5.25% from 5%, which was announced just hours before fourth-quarter inflation proved to be much less than expected. \par \par \uc2  Brash said he has negotiated a new agreement with the new center-left coalition government, and understands the government wants to avoid the sudden appreciation in the New Zealand dollar that occurred in the 1990s and which proved so painful for exporters. \par \par \uc2  He said it is in everyone's interest to avoid such rapid local dollar appreciation. \par \par \uc2  The increases in the official cash rate in November and January were the best way to avoid the risk of a repeat of those rapid local dollar rises because it "nabs inflation before it builds a full head of steam." \par \par \uc2  However, Brash said he can't guarantee the strategy. \par \par \uc2  Brash told the committee that he sees three potential advantages arising from the New Zealand government's proposal to sponsor an independent inquiry into the \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  of monetary policy in New Zealand. \par \par \uc2  One possible outcome from the proposed inquiry, details of which Finance Minister Michael Cullen is expected to outline later this month, is that it will affirm or endorse the country's existing framework for conducting monetary policy, he said. \par \par \uc2  A second possible outcome of the inquiry is that it will offer useful advice on whether the Reserve Bank is fully utilizing all of the instruments available to it to influence monetary conditions. \par \par \uc2  He said it would be useful to have an independent authority express a view on whether the Reserve Bank ought to, for example, attempt a greater control over credit supply within the economy. \par \par \uc2  Brash also noted the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, but said it would still be useful for an independent person to lend an assessment on that stance. \par \par \uc2  A third possible outcome of the inquiry, Brash said, is that it may find that there is room to improve the internal structures of the New Zealand \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which deliberates and make decisions on monetary policy \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Brash also said he thinks it would be better to have a non-New\par Zealander conduct the inquiry.\par    -By Chris Ritchie; Dow Jones Newswires; 644-8018960;critchie@ap.org\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw2f00w4w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
