\par\par \b \uc2  Concerns about RBNZ independence overblown - NZPA. \b0\par\par\uc2 465 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Januar 2000\par 01:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WELLINGTON, Jan 21 (Reuters) - Offshore investors have turned away from New Zealand because of a perceived dispute between Finance Minister Michael Cullen and the Reserve Bank which some believe may undermine the independence of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the New Zealand Press Association reported on Friday. \par \par \uc2  But a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer said the perception that the bank's independence was under threat was exaggerated, NZPA reported. \par \par \uc2  Concern has arisen since the Reserve Bank increased its official cash rate on Wednesday, to 5.25 percent from 5.00 percent, only a couple of hours before the release of surprisingly low December quarter inflation figures. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Michael Cullen, not yet two months in the job since his leftist coalition took power, then said he would meet with bank governor Don Brash next week to discuss the timing of the hike and the accuracy of the bank's and Treasury's forecasts. \par \par \uc2  He also said he expected in future the bank would "not necessarily assume inflationary pressures are there when the published figures are showing they are not". \par \par \uc2  "Comments such as that have made the offshore market slightly jittery about how \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  the new Government may wish to be," Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Greg Ball told NZPA. \par \par \uc2  Adding to foreign investor jumpiness is that just after his appointment Cullen altered the central Policy Targets Agreement with the Reserve Bank. It was a minor change but was read as an indication that the new Government wanted the bank to pay more attention to economic growth, rather than just inflation. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, the stock market's key NZSE-40 capital index lost 1.74 percent as foreigners sold in response to the rate rise. \par \par \uc2  This morning, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was down more than a third of a US cent at 51.41 US cents from 51.78 US cents shortly before Wednesday morning's interest rate move. Overnight, the kiwi hit 51.28 US cents. \par \par \uc2  Economists are divided over whether Cullen should have spoken publicly, but Ball said foreigners were acting on fear of the unknown. \par \par \uc2  "It's been over-reported, it's been over-emphasised," Ball said. "I don't think it is that important." \par \par \uc2  Ball said the market was concerned next week's meeting would see Cullen telling Brash how to run monetary policy - anathema to markets which watched New Zealand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  lead the way in formalising independence from Government interference. \par \par \uc2  "There's no reason to believe that," Ball said. "Personally, I don't put a lot of credence on that. All this is is a change of government, and people do things slightly differently." \par \par \uc2  ((Wellington newsroom, +64 4 471 4277, fax +64 4 473 6212; wellington.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw1l0207w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
