\par\par \b \uc2  NZ/Brash/ \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Union -3: Central Bk Doesn't  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 177 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juni 1999\par 02:52\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Brash said in the decade between 1988 and 1998, the New Zealand dollar was less volatile against the U.S. dollar than the Japanese, German or British \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were over the same period. \par \par \uc2  The lower volatility may in part be due to the fact the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, and may also be partly because of financial market participants' understanding of the role of the exchange rate in determining future price movements in New Zealand, he said. \par \par \uc2  "We don't change interest rates or monetary policy in order to achieve a particular exchange-rate outcome, but if the exchange rate moves, either up or down, we take that into account as a factor which may affect our inflation rate. Because financial markets understand this point, this may tend to stabilize the exchange rate that, as in all small open economies, has important direct and indirect effects on the inflation rate," Brash said. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 22-06-99 \par \par \uc2  0052GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv6m04qso\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
