\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-NZ dollar assets win friends, for now. \b0\par\par\uc2  By James Gray \par \uc2 619 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juni 1998\par 02:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  AUCKLAND, June 18 (Reuters) - New Zealand dollar assets won some unlikely friends on Thursday, courtesy of Japanese and U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yen, but analysts doubted the friendship would last. \par \par \uc2  The New Zealand dollar's slippery slide over the last year has accelerated in recent weeks thanks to the likelihood of a credit rating downgrade by Moody's Investors Service and continued concerns about the large current account deficit. \par \par \uc2  The weak yen compounded the kiwi's woes until Wednesday night (NZ time), when Japan and the U.S. stepped in to shore up the yen, and the kiwi rallied more than two U.S. cents. \par \par \uc2  By noon the kiwi was holding onto most of its gains, trading at US$0.5188/95 from 0.4974/81 at Wednesday's close. \par \par \uc2  Market commentators were unsure whether \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would work over the longer term and whether Japan's economic reform package would achieve what it set out to. \par \par \uc2  But the kiwi's reprieve and a sharply stronger Wall Street brought a sharp rally in New Zealand equities, the benchmark NZSE-40 Capital Index gaining almost two percent to 2,038.21. \par \par \uc2  Wholesale interest rates - which had been under pressure from the lower kiwi - fell back sharply. \par \par \uc2  By late morning, key 90-day bank bill rates were at 9.25 percent from almost 9.70 percent at Wednesday's close. \par \par \uc2  Ulf Schoefisch, chief economist at Deutsche Morgan Grenfell NZ, said there was no doubt about the degree of determination behind the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "I think the market will not try to push the yen lower again in the short term, but as we have seen in previous periods of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the markets have taken three to four weeks to break through the levels again," he said. \par \par \uc2  "So ultimately I think we will see a weaker yen again, but it may take a while." \par \par \uc2  While the kiwi had benefited, downside risk would open up again should the yen suffer a relapse. "The fundamentals have not changed, neither here nor in Japan," Schoefisch said. \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Jenny Shipley was also cautious. \par \par \uc2  "What has happened overnight is helpful, but I will only personally have real confidence in whether or not we are going to find our way out if we see the sorts of reforms undertaken in Japan that not only I but very many other APEC leaders have discussed on many occasions in the recent past," she said. \par \par \uc2  Vicki St John at share brokers Credit Suisse First Boston said the recovery in equities looked brittle. \par \par \uc2  "At this stage it still feels fragile and vulnerable to any reasonable changes in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . If the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  whipped back down again we would react pretty quickly," she said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers agreed it was too early to say whether kiwi's downtrend had finished so soon after plumbing 12-year lows against the greenback. \par \par \uc2  "We've got to see what happens in Japan. If they don't do anything fundamental - if it is solely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - then it won't last," said one kiwi dealer. \par \par \uc2  While \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had put a gloss on \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets for the time being, the fundamentals for the economy were still weak - private forecasters expect data due on June 26 to show flat growth in the March quarter. \par \par \uc2  And the March quarter current account defifit - the primary cause of Moody's concern - is expected to climb to about 8.0 percent of GDP in the March quarter from 7.7 percent in December. That data is due on June 24. \par \par \uc2  ((Wellington newsroom, +64 4 4734746, fax +64 4 4736212; wellington.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6i02r3z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
