\par\par \b \uc2  RBNZ's hands seen tied by kiwi dollar's weakness. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Simon Louisson \par \uc2 592 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Juni 1998\par 06:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WELLINGTON, June 9 (Reuters) - New Zealand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has little option but to let the kiwi dollar continue its downward spiral, analysts said on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank on Wednesday morning faces the first major test of its recently introduced policy of only commenting or altering monetary conditions after its weekly Monetary Policy Committee meeting on Tuesdays. \par \par \uc2  Analysts believe the bank is hamstrung in using its major monetary policy lever - a change in the daily cash target - because of the fragile state of the economy. \par \par \uc2  A tightening at this stage could send the economy into reverse. \par \par \uc2  Conversely, if the bank does not want conditions to ease any more it is largely powerless. \par \par \uc2  Silence from the bank on Wednesday morning would be tantamount to sanctioning easier conditions, said Deutsche Morgan Grenfell Bank economist Ulf Schoefish. \par \par \uc2  "It's all conditional on where the Monetary Conditions Index is. It might just sneak in to a level that is acceptable to the Reserve Bank," he said. \par \par \uc2  Monetary conditions have eased to over a hundred basis points below the RBNZ's target for the September quarter, as outlined by the bank in its May 26 statement. \par \par \uc2  The index was at 245 on Tuesday afternoon compared with the bank's current target of 350. \par \par \uc2  Last week, traders interpreted the RBNZ's silence after its monetary conditions meeting as effectively sanctioning the MCI at about 90 points below the 350 target. \par \par \uc2  The New Zealand dollar has fallen over two U.S. cents since the bank's May 26 set-piece and, apart from a short spike down in January 1993, is now at its lowest level since late 1986. \par \par \uc2  The RBNZ has never \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since it was floated in 1985 and Governor Don Brash is on record as stating that he believes \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be largely futile. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders believe the Reserve Bank of Australia has spent upwards of A$3 billion defending the Aussie, which plunged towards an all-time low on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Australian weakness has been a big factor in the New Zealand dollar's fall with both countries catching cold from Asia. \par \par \uc2  Schoefish believes there is a zero chance of the RBNZ lowering its cash target, due to the weak state of the economy. \par \par \uc2  He said the problem for the bank was its inability to direct the mix of monetary conditions to ensure any easing came via either the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  or interest rates, but not the other. \par \par \uc2  BNZ Chief \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer Mike Symonds said that even if the RBNZ declined to comment, he doubted the market would ease too aggressively from this point. \par \par \uc2  "The MCI looks as though it will settle around the minus-90, minus-100 level but up towards minus-130 and minus-135 we are pushing the outer limits," he said. \par \par \uc2  The kiwi market would again take its lead from global \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets overnight, he said. \par \par \uc2  "Given the way the Aussie is performing, the outlook is not particularly positive. But the kiwi is holding in there, just," he said. \par \par \uc2  Treasurer Winston Peters firmly ruled out any question of the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  government supporting \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "We are not getting into the business of risking the taxpayer's dollar against the investment decisions of thousands of people abroad in the way that Australia has," he told National Radio in an interview during the morning. \par \par \uc2  ((Wellington newsroom +64 4 4734 746, fax +64 4 4736 212, wellington.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6903gkm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
