\par\par \b \uc2  NZ ministers see kiwi fall as normal market action. \b0\par\par\uc2 496 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Juni 1998\par 00:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  AUCKLAND, June 9 (Reuters) - New Zealand's Finance Minister Bill Birch and Treasurer Winston Peters on Tuesday played down the significance of the recent sharp fall in the kiwi dollar, saying it showed international markets at work and was not going to spur extraordinary policy action such as \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Birch, in Auckland, told Reuters he felt the movement in the New Zealand dollar was the result of "the market at work, and our framework accommodates that. \par \par \uc2  "We've got a robust framework of economic policies and I'm sure that's the best way to allow those adjustments to take place," he added. \par \par \uc2  Earlier, speaking on National Radio, Peters firmly ruled out any question of the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  government supporting \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  In recent weeks the Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Reserve Bank of Australia (RBA), has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the tumbling Aussie dollar. \par \par \uc2  "We are not getting into the business of risking the taxpayer's dollar against the investment decisions of thousands of people abroad in the way that Australia has," Peters said in a morning radio interview. \par \par \uc2  Birch, speaking after giving a speech to a gas industry conference in Auckland, said he did not think the Reserve Bank of New Zealand (RBNZ) would be especially shocked by the movement in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  either, given that it was mainly driven by prevailing trends in international markets including the U.S. dollar's significant strengthening against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Asked if he expected the RBNZ to comment on Wednesday morning after its weekly monetary poilicy meeting, Birch said: "I'm sure that the Reserve Bank will be monitoring it closely, but I don't think that the way international \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets are working are a surprise to the Reserve Bank." \par \par \uc2  Peters said another factor in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fall was that "we did have an over-rated dollar and it will probably settle at a far more acceptable level". \par \par \uc2  The Treasurer has said on several occasions that he considered the dollar to be over-valued in 1996 into 1997 and has welcomed the steady depreciation seen since the middle of last year. \par \par \uc2  He said he did not believe there had been any capital flight from New Zealand associated with its latest fall. \par \par \uc2  "It may well have stabilised for all we know," he added. \par \par \uc2  Asked if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fall could justify the government abandoning its inflation target, Peters said the target had been widened after the 1996 election to a band of nought to three percent from nought to two. \par \par \uc2  He said the wider target allowed a far more flexible, long-term response from the governor of the Reserve Bank who would not have to make knee-jerk reactions of the type the bank could be forced into with the narrower target. \par \par \uc2  ((Wellington newsroom +64 4 4734 746, fax +64 4 4736 212, wellington.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6803e9d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
