\par\par \b \uc2  FEATURE - New Zealand inflation-tamer in control. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Simon Louisson \par \uc2 1,106 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Oktober 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WELLINGTON, Oct 19 (Reuter) - The man who has largely driven inflation out of New Zealand, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Don Brash, is far happier now he has curbed the annual rate back within a government-set target of 0-2 percent. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, New Zealand's annual underlying inflation fell to two percent from 2.2 percent at June 30 and Brash celebrated by sending out an easing signal which sent short-term interest yields scurrying 50 basis points downwards. \par \par \uc2  Asked in an interview on Wednesday if he still enjoys the job after seven years, Brash said: "I enjoy it far more when I am inside the range than when I am outside the range." \par \par \uc2  The very tall, 55 year-old Brash said he had no plans to retire voluntarily. \par \par \uc2  He is not quite half way through a five year contract he signs with the government which gives him \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  independence to keep inflation within the agreed range and gives the finance minister the right to fire him if he fails. \par \par \uc2  New Zealand had inflation in the mid-teens when Brash was appointed in 1988, but since passing the Reserve Bank of New Zealand (RBNZ) Act 1989 has had one of the lowest rates in the world. \par \par \uc2  Twice the target band has been breached -- once in 1991 when the band was undershot during the very rapid disinflation process, and then in the June 1995 quarter when it was overshot. \par \par \uc2  Brash believes the act has delivered to New Zealand an "astonishingly stable" \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , lower interest rates than most developed countries as well as the low inflation rate. \par \par \uc2  While the act says nothing about economic growth targets, GDP in the June year rose 5.5 percent and Brash believes a rate of 3.5 percent-plus is sustainable. \par \par \uc2  Unemployment has also dropped dramatically, to 6.3 percent from over 11 percent in the early 1990s. \par \par \uc2  "If you had said 10 years ago that a country as small as New Zealand, without Switzerland's track record, would ever get its 10-year bonds under U.S. 10-year rates you would have been certified," Brash said. \par \par \uc2  (Ten year bonds now above U.S. rates at 7.14 percent.) \par \par \uc2  "I find it hard to think what other reason there might be (than the act)," he said. \par \par \uc2  He pointed to the fact that although the much larger economy of Australia had a lower ratio of public debt to GDP, its bond yields have been 100-200 basis points above New Zealand's for some years. \par \par \uc2  "You would have to conclude the reason for the substantial gap has to do with the credibilty of the legislation." \par \par \uc2  STEADY "KIWI" DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  While there has been huge volatility in even major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent years, New Zealand's curency has mostly maintained a gradual upward path. And since the "Kiwi" dollar floated 10 years ago, the RBNZ has never had to directly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar has sailed along within a remarkably narrow range," said Brash of the Kiwi's recent performance. \par \par \uc2  The Trade-Weighted Index (TWI) measuring the Kiwi against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  has moved in a 59.0-62.75 band in the past seven months. \par \par \uc2  The RBNZ indirectly targets the TWI and interest rates as they impact on price stabilty and monetary policy but it sets no particular ranges. \par \par \uc2  "If you have a very precise inflation target and you are a small, open economy inevitably you have to form a judgement within which the exchange rate can move and still be consistant with inflation in the target band," Brash said. \par \par \uc2  Opposition Labour Party finance spokesman Michael Cullen said that the RBNZ's use of the exchange rate to contain domestic prices unfairly hits the tradeable sector rather than service industries where most inflationary pressures have appeared. \par \par \uc2  However, Brash said there is not much he can do about that. \par \par \uc2  "I sometimes wish that I could control the mix of interest rates and the exchange but I have long since accepted that I can't," he said. "We can't control the mix, the market controls that. We can only tighten or ease." \par \par \uc2  HIGH REAL INTEREST RATES \par \par \uc2  Some critics of the RBNZ said that New Zealand appears doomed to have high real interest rates. Home lending rates are 10.5 percent compared with the two percent inflation rate. \par \par \uc2  "We have high real interest rates now because we had 20 years of high inflation and negative real interest rates so that savers got so badly burned that they haven't got used to the idea of accepting three to four percent on deposit as a prudent thing to do." \par \par \uc2  Other critics contend that high real rates choke off growth and every time the economy appears ready to take off, the RBNZ, fearful of inflation pressures, forces interest rates up. \par \par \uc2  The experience of five to six percent economic growth over the past two years which has pushed inflation outside the barrier has taught the RBNZ that New Zealand's sustainable growth rate is somewhat below that. \par \par \uc2  Some economists believe a rate of 3.5 percent is aiming too low when compared to countries like Singapore. \par \par \uc2  But Brash said that Singapore now sees its sustainable rate at four to six percent while the U.S. sees its sustainable rate at 2.5 percent and Britain at 2.25 percent. \par \par \uc2  "So 3.5 is quite respectable by the standards of other developed countries with which we often compare outselves." \par \par \uc2  The RBNZ forecasts New Zealand's growth will slow from 5.5 percent at June 30 to two percent next March year and then, boosted by planned government tax cuts, 3.3 percent in 1997. \par \par \uc2  Former RBNZ financial markets manager and now ANZ Bank New Zealand Treasurer, Grant Spencer, recently called for a review of the 0-2 percent inflation band. He suggested the RBNZ could avoid sharp policy changes as inflation nears edges of the band. \par \par \uc2  But Brash believes central banks with a wider target tend to react too late and the winding back costs more in terms of lost production and social costs. \par \par \uc2  Also he said New Zealanders are getting used to two percent. \par \par \uc2  "In New Zealand 0-2 has become symbolic of the commitment of successive governments to price stability. Any change would be perceived of a weakening of that commitment even if that was not the intention." \par \par \uc2  He remains confident that whatever the result of the election due next year under a new mixed member proportional system, a majority of members will support the Reserve Bank Act. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103drai02sdi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
